\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces The effect of the number of fields present in the rubric used to extract a structured summary of the trace on MAE for different number of executed tasks and different number of reference systems for both task-specific and generic rubrics. We report both the MAE for the average (bold) and best performing (thin) sets of fields. In general, a task-specific rubric is preferred to a generic rubric when there are fewer reference systems and more executed tasks available.}}{9}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sensitivity}{{6}{9}{The effect of the number of fields present in the rubric used to extract a structured summary of the trace on MAE for different number of executed tasks and different number of reference systems for both task-specific and generic rubrics. We report both the MAE for the average (bold) and best performing (thin) sets of fields. In general, a task-specific rubric is preferred to a generic rubric when there are fewer reference systems and more executed tasks available}{figure.6}{}}
